    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 pb-16 lg:pb-24">
      <div class="prose prose-lg prose-blue max-w-none text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
        <p class="text-2xl font-medium text-gray-900 mb-8 italic">
          Under the plastic hood at the front of your treadmill sits a motor, a controller board and
          mains wiring, and a fan pulling room air across all three. Everything that fan draws in
          that is not air stays there, and after a few years it is a blanket over the component
          least able to tolerate one.
        </p>

        <div id="why-it-matters" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">1. Why This Task Matters</h2>
<p>
            Treadmill motors are air-cooled. A fan draws room air through vents in the hood, across
            the motor housing and the controller board, and out again. That airflow is the entire
            thermal management system, and it depends on the path staying clear.
          </p>
          <p>
            Dust does two things to it. It settles as an insulating layer on the motor housing and
            on the board's heat sinks, so heat generated inside cannot escape into the air. And it
            accumulates in the vents and around the fan, reducing the volume of air moving through
            at all. The two compound: less airflow over a better-insulated surface.
          </p>
          <p>
            Heat is the primary failure mechanism for DC treadmill motors and for controller boards.
            A motor running ten degrees hotter than designed does not fail today; it fails
            noticeably earlier than it should have. Since this is the single most expensive component
            in most treadmills, ten minutes a year is a straightforward trade.
          </p>
          <p>
            There is a second reason. A thick dust layer on a board can hold moisture and create
            tracking paths between contacts, and in homes with pets a compacted mat of hair around a
            warm motor is a genuine fire consideration rather than a theoretical one.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="safety" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">2. The Safety Rules — Read These First</h2>
<p>
            <strong>Unplug at the wall.</strong> Not the safety key, which only interrupts the
            control circuit. Physically remove the plug from the socket and put it where you can
            see it.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Wait at least thirty minutes after unplugging</strong> before opening the hood.
            Capacitors on the controller board store charge and do not discharge the instant power
            is removed. Waiting costs nothing.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Do not touch the controller board.</strong> Not with the vacuum nozzle, not
            with a brush, not with a finger. Vacuum near it, never against it. Board components are
            fragile and static-sensitive, and a nozzle knocking a component off is an expensive
            mistake with no upside.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Do not disconnect anything.</strong> No wires, no connectors, no ground straps.
            You are cleaning, not servicing.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Do not use liquid of any kind</strong> inside the compartment — no sprays, no
            damp cloths, no cleaners.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>If you see damage, stop.</strong> Scorch marks, melted plastic, a burnt smell,
            corroded contacts or rodent damage all mean close the hood and call an engineer rather
            than continuing.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="should-you" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">3. Should You Do This Yourself?</h2>
<p>
            For most people, yes. Removing a plastic hood held by a handful of screws and vacuuming
            without touching anything is well within ordinary competence, and the risk with the
            machine unplugged and rested is low.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Do it yourself if</strong> you are comfortable with a screwdriver, you can
            follow the rule about not touching the board, and the machine is out of warranty or the
            manual describes this as owner maintenance — many do.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Do not do it yourself if</strong> the machine is under warranty and the manual
            says opening the hood voids it, you are unsure about working near mains-connected
            components, or you find anything on the damage list above when you look.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>The middle option</strong> is worth knowing: if you have an engineer visiting
            for any reason, ask them to clean the compartment while they are there. It adds minutes
            to a visit you are already paying for.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="what-you-need" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">4. What You Will Need</h2>
<p>
            <strong>A screwdriver</strong> matching the hood fasteners — usually Phillips, sometimes
            a hex key, occasionally a security-star bit on newer machines. Check before you start
            rather than halfway through.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>A vacuum with a narrow crevice tool</strong> and ideally a soft brush
            attachment. A handheld vacuum is easier to control in a confined space than a full-size
            cylinder.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>A soft, dry paintbrush</strong> — one to two inches wide — for loosening dust
            from surfaces the nozzle cannot reach. Dry only.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>A phone with a torch</strong>, for lighting the compartment and for taking a
            photograph before you start. The photo is genuinely useful: it records where wires run
            and which fastener came from where.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>A small container</strong> for the screws. Hood fasteners are often different
            lengths and they roll.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="step-one" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">5. Step One: Unplug, Wait, and Open</h2>
<p>
            Unplug at the wall and leave the machine for thirty minutes. Use the time to gather
            tools.
          </p>
          <p>
            If the machine folds, leave it flat and lower — you want the hood accessible and the
            machine stable, not raised. Some designs are easier to reach with the deck folded up;
            check yours, but never work under a folded deck that is not positively locked.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Locate the hood fasteners.</strong> Typically four to eight screws around the
            edge of the plastic cover at the front, sometimes with a couple underneath the leading
            edge. The manual will show them.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Remove them into your container</strong> and note any difference in length. Lift
            the hood straight up and away. If it resists, look for a fastener you have missed rather
            than forcing it — the plastic is easier to break than it looks, and lugs at the front
            edge often need the cover lifting at a slight angle.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Take a photograph</strong> before touching anything.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="step-two" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">6. Step Two: Look Before You Vacuum</h2>
<p>
            Spend a minute looking, because this is the one annual view you get of these components
            and it is diagnostically useful.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Note the dust distribution.</strong> Even, light dust across everything is
            normal. A heavy concentration around the fan intake suggests the machine sits somewhere
            dusty. A dense mat of fibre points to carpet, and our
            <a href="/treadmill-on-carpet/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">carpet guide</a>
            covers why that matters.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Look for the damage list:</strong> scorch marks or discoloration on the board,
            melted or deformed plastic, a burnt smell, green or white corrosion on contacts, chewed
            insulation or droppings. Any of these means stop, replace the hood, and call an
            engineer.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Check the drive belt</strong> — the toothed belt from motor to front roller —
            for fraying, cracking or obvious slack. You are not adjusting it, only noting its
            condition.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Check that wires are routed clear</strong> of the drive belt and any moving
            part, and that nothing is pinched.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="step-three" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">7. Step Three: Vacuum, Without Touching</h2>
<p>
            The technique is deliberate rather than thorough. Work from the top down and from the
            outside in, so dislodged dust falls into areas you have not yet cleaned.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Hold the nozzle slightly away from surfaces</strong> rather than dragging it
            across them. You are collecting airborne and loose dust, not scrubbing. Around the
            controller board specifically, keep an inch of clearance and never let the nozzle
            contact it.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Use the soft brush to loosen dust</strong> from the motor housing and the frame
            with one hand while holding the nozzle nearby with the other. Do not brush the board.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Prioritise three areas:</strong> the fan and its intake vents, which is where
            restriction matters most; the motor housing and any cooling fins; and the floor of the
            compartment, where everything eventually settles.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Do not use compressed air.</strong> It drives dust deeper into the motor
            windings and into bearings rather than removing it, and it can generate static near the
            board. This is the most common piece of bad advice on this subject.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="step-four" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">8. Step Four: Close It Up</h2>
<p>
            <strong>Check nothing has moved.</strong> Compare against your photograph — wires in
            the same routing, no connector nudged loose, nothing resting against the drive belt.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Refit the hood</strong>, engaging any locating lugs before starting screws.
            Start every fastener by hand a couple of turns before tightening any of them, which
            avoids cross-threading and stops the cover sitting skewed.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Tighten firmly, not hard.</strong> These are self-tapping screws into plastic
            bosses and they strip easily. Snug is correct.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Plug in and run the machine unloaded at a walking pace for two or three
            minutes.</strong> Listen for anything new — a rattle usually means a loose fastener or
            the hood not fully seated. Then walk on it briefly and confirm normal behaviour before
            a full session.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="how-often" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">9. How Often to Do This</h2>
<p>
            <strong>Annually</strong> for a machine in a normal heated room on a hard floor. That is
            the baseline in our
            <a href="/treadmill-maintenance-checklist/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">maintenance checklist</a>.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Every six months</strong> if the machine sits on carpet, lives in a garage,
            basement or workshop, or shares a home with a shedding pet. Carpet is the single largest
            factor — pile sheds continuously and the fan is pointed at it.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Sooner, regardless of schedule,</strong> if the motor sounds like it is working
            harder than it used to, if the compartment area feels hot after a session, or if the
            machine has recently moved from a clean room to a dusty one.
          </p>
          <p>
            Note that a motor working harder is more often a friction problem than a cooling one.
            Lubricate first — our
            <a href="/treadmill-belt-lubrication/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">lubrication guide</a>
            covers it — and treat the compartment clean as the second step rather than the first.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="what-not-to-do" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">10. What Not to Do In There</h2>
<p>
            The list of things to avoid is short and worth stating explicitly, because most of them
            appear in well-meaning advice online.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Do not lubricate anything.</strong> The motor bearings are sealed and the drive
            belt is designed to run dry. Oil in this compartment attracts dust into a paste and can
            cause the drive belt to slip.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Do not adjust the drive belt tension</strong> unless you know what you are
            doing and the manual describes it. Over-tightening loads the motor bearings directly.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Do not disturb wiring or connectors</strong>, however untidy it looks.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Do not use any liquid, spray or cleaner.</strong>
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Do not adjust anything on the controller board.</strong> Some boards have
            calibration potentiometers. They are set correctly and there is nothing to gain.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="verdict" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">11. The Short Version</h2>
<p>
            Unplug at the wall and wait thirty minutes. Remove the hood screws into a container,
            lift the hood, and photograph the inside.
          </p>
          <p>
            Look for scorch marks, melted plastic, corrosion or rodent damage — if you find any,
            stop and call an engineer. Otherwise vacuum with a crevice tool held slightly clear of
            surfaces, loosening dust with a dry soft brush, prioritising the fan intake, the motor
            housing and the compartment floor. Never touch the controller board and never use
            compressed air or liquid.
          </p>
          <p>
            Refit the hood, start all screws by hand before tightening, and run the machine
            unloaded for a few minutes listening for anything new.
          </p>
          <p>
            Ten minutes, once a year — six-monthly on carpet or in a garage. It is the most skipped
            task in treadmill maintenance and it protects the most expensive component in the
            machine.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>


      <div class="prose prose-lg prose-blue max-w-none text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">

        <!-- FAQ -->
        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">12. Frequently Asked Questions</h2>
          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How do you clean a treadmill motor compartment?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Unplug at the wall, wait thirty minutes for capacitors to discharge, remove the hood screws and lift the cover. Vacuum with a narrow crevice tool held slightly clear of surfaces, using a dry soft brush to loosen dust, prioritising the fan intake, motor housing and compartment floor. Never touch the controller board.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is it safe to open a treadmill motor cover?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                For most people, yes, provided the machine is unplugged at the wall — not just switched off with the safety key — and left thirty minutes before opening, since controller boards retain charge. Do not touch the board, do not disconnect anything, and use no liquids. If the machine is under warranty, check whether opening the hood voids it.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How often should you vacuum a treadmill motor compartment?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Annually for a machine in a normal heated room on a hard floor. Every six months if it sits on carpet, lives in a garage or basement, or shares a home with a shedding pet. Carpet is the largest single factor because the pile sheds continuously and the cooling fan is pointed at it.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can I use compressed air to clean a treadmill motor?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                No. Compressed air drives dust deeper into the motor windings and bearings instead of removing it, and it can generate static near the controller board. A vacuum takes material away rather than relocating it, which is the whole point of the exercise.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Does dust in the motor compartment cause overheating?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Yes, in two ways at once. Dust settles as an insulating layer on the motor housing and heat sinks so heat cannot escape into the air, and it clogs the vents and fan so less air moves through at all. Heat is the primary failure mechanism for DC motors and controller boards.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What should I do if I see scorch marks inside my treadmill?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Stop, refit the hood and call a qualified engineer. Scorch marks, melted or deformed plastic, a burnt smell, corroded contacts or rodent damage all indicate a fault that cleaning will not address, and continuing to use the machine risks making it considerably worse.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div class="mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">Common Mistakes Cleaning a Motor Compartment</h2>
          <p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">Two of these are safety errors and two will cost you money. All four appear regularly in advice online.</p>
          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Relying on the safety key instead of unplugging</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">The safety key interrupts the control circuit, not the mains supply. Physically remove the plug from the wall socket and leave it visible. Then wait thirty minutes before opening the hood, because capacitors on the controller board store charge and do not discharge the moment power is removed.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Touching the controller board</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">Board components are fragile and static-sensitive, and a vacuum nozzle knocking one off is an expensive mistake with no upside. Vacuum near the board with an inch of clearance, never against it, and never brush it. If dust is stubbornly attached to the board, leave it — the cost of a mistake far exceeds the benefit.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Using compressed air</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">It drives dust into the motor windings and bearings rather than removing it, and can generate static near the board. This is the most common piece of bad advice on the subject, probably because it works for computers, where the geometry and the components are quite different.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Lubricating something while you are in there</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">The motor bearings are sealed and the drive belt is designed to run dry. Oil in this compartment collects dust into an abrasive paste and can make the drive belt slip. The only lubrication a treadmill needs is silicone between the belt and the deck, which is a completely separate job.</p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

